🔐 Kubernetes RBAC (Role-Based Access Control)

RBAC (Role-Based Access Control) is a Kubernetes authorization mechanism used to control what users, groups, and service accounts are allowed to do within a Kubernetes cluster.

Instead of giving every user full access to the cluster, RBAC allows administrators to define specific permissions based on roles.

🧠 Core idea
User / ServiceAccount
        ↓
    RoleBinding
        ↓
       Role
        ↓
    Permissions

For cluster-wide permissions:

User / ServiceAccount
        ↓
ClusterRoleBinding
        ↓
   ClusterRole
        ↓
   Permissions

RBAC follows the Principle of Least Privilege, meaning users and applications should receive only the permissions they actually need.

📌 What does RBAC control?

RBAC permissions are generally based on three things:

WHO → Subject
WHAT → Resource
ACTION → Verb

For example:

User: afsar
Resource: Pods
Action: get

means:

User afsar is allowed to retrieve Pods.

Common verbs include:

get
list
watch
create
update
patch
delete
1️⃣ Role

A Role defines permissions for resources within a specific namespace.

Example:

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role

metadata:
  name: pod-reader
  namespace: development

rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]

This Role says:

In the development namespace, the subject can get, list, and watch Pods.

It does not give access to Pods in every namespace.

Important:
Role
 ↓
Namespace-scoped permissions
2️⃣ RoleBinding

A RoleBinding connects a Role to a user, group, or ServiceAccount.

The Role defines what permissions exist, while the RoleBinding determines who receives those permissions.

Example:

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding

metadata:
  name: pod-reader-binding
  namespace: development

subjects:
  - kind: User
    name: developer
    apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

roleRef:
  kind: Role
  name: pod-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

Now:

developer
    ↓
RoleBinding
    ↓
pod-reader Role
    ↓
get/list/watch Pods
    ↓
development namespace
🧠 Easy way to remember

Role = What can be done?
RoleBinding = Who can do it?

3️⃣ ClusterRole

A ClusterRole defines permissions that can be used at the cluster level or for namespaced resources across namespaces.

Example:

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRole

metadata:
  name: pod-reader

rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]

A ClusterRole can define permissions for:

Pods
Services
Deployments
Nodes
Namespaces
PersistentVolumes
etc.

Unlike a Role, ClusterRole is not limited to one namespace.

For example, permissions can include cluster-scoped resources such as:

Nodes
Namespaces
PersistentVolumes
4️⃣ ClusterRoleBinding

A ClusterRoleBinding connects a ClusterRole to a user, group, or ServiceAccount at the cluster level.

Example:

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: ClusterRoleBinding

metadata:
  name: pod-reader-binding

subjects:
  - kind: User
    name: developer
    apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

roleRef:
  kind: ClusterRole
  name: pod-reader
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io

This gives the developer user the permissions defined in the ClusterRole across the cluster's namespaces where those permissions apply.

🔥 Role vs ClusterRole
Role	ClusterRole
Namespace-scoped	Cluster-scoped definition
Used for permissions within a namespace	Can define cluster-wide or namespaced-resource permissions
Cannot directly grant access to cluster-scoped resources	Can include cluster-scoped resources
Example: Pods in dev namespace	Example: Nodes across cluster
🔥 RoleBinding vs ClusterRoleBinding
RoleBinding	ClusterRoleBinding
Grants a Role or ClusterRole within a namespace	Grants a ClusterRole cluster-wide
Namespace-specific binding	Cluster-wide binding
Useful for developers working in one namespace	Useful for cluster-level administrators/operators
Important interview point ⭐

A RoleBinding can reference a ClusterRole.

For example:

ClusterRole
    ↓
RoleBinding
    ↓
specific namespace

This means you can reuse a ClusterRole's permission definition but grant those permissions only within the namespace where the RoleBinding exists.

🧩 Subjects in RBAC

RBAC can grant permissions to three main types of subjects:

User
kind: User
name: developer

Represents a human user.

Group
kind: Group
name: developers

Represents a group of users.

ServiceAccount
kind: ServiceAccount
name: backend-sa

Represents an identity used by applications/Pods running inside Kubernetes.

🔑 RBAC follows Least Privilege

One of the most important security concepts I learned is the Principle of Least Privilege.

Instead of:

Developer → Full cluster admin ❌

we should provide only what is required:

Developer
   ↓
Can view Pods
Can view Services
Can view Deployments
   ↓
Cannot delete resources
Cannot modify cluster configuration

This reduces the impact of accidental or unauthorized actions.

🔍 Checking RBAC Permissions

Kubernetes provides:

kubectl auth can-i

Example:

kubectl auth can-i get pods

Check whether a user can delete Pods:

kubectl auth can-i delete pods

Check permissions in a specific namespace:

kubectl auth can-i get pods -n development

You can also check another user:

kubectl auth can-i get pods --as=developer

For example:

yes

means the requested action is allowed.

no

means it is denied.

🔄 Complete RBAC Flow

The whole concept can be visualized as:

                    RBAC
                     │
                     ↓
                 SUBJECT
            ┌────────┼────────┐
            ↓        ↓        ↓
          User     Group   ServiceAccount
                     │
                     ↓
                 BINDING
              ┌──────┴──────┐
              ↓             ↓
        RoleBinding    ClusterRoleBinding
              ↓             ↓
            Role       ClusterRole
              │             │
              └──────┬──────┘
                     ↓
                Permissions
                     ↓
             Resource + Verb

Example:

developer
    ↓
RoleBinding
    ↓
Role: pod-reader
    ↓
Pods
    ↓
get / list / watch
🚀 Real-World Example

Suppose a company has:

Production Namespace
Development Namespace
Monitoring Namespace

A developer only needs to view Pods in development.

Instead of giving:

cluster-admin ❌

we create:

Role
 ↓
get/list/watch Pods
 ↓
development namespace

and:

RoleBinding
 ↓
developer

Result:

Developer
   │
   ├── development Pods → get/list/watch ✅
   ├── production Pods  → ❌
   ├── Nodes             → ❌
   └── Cluster settings  → ❌

This is a practical implementation of least privilege.

📝 Key Takeaways
RBAC
│
├── Role
│   └── Defines namespace-scoped permissions
│
├── RoleBinding
│   └── Assigns Role/ClusterRole permissions within a namespace
│
├── ClusterRole
│   └── Defines cluster-scoped or reusable permissions
│
└── ClusterRoleBinding
    └── Grants ClusterRole permissions cluster-wide
🧠 Four things to remember:

Role → What permissions?

RoleBinding → Who gets those permissions?

ClusterRole → Cluster-level/reusable permission definition

ClusterRoleBinding → Who gets ClusterRole permissions cluster-wide?

And the overall purpose:

RBAC controls access to Kubernetes resources by associating subjects with permissions, helping enforce least privilege and improve cluster security. 🔐☸️